    "summary": "Scorching Viola Lee Blues and tight Good Lovin' jam",
    "blurb": "Opening with a punchy Casey Jones, the band moves through an acoustic set featuring deep blues numbers before launching into a high‑energy electric second set highlighted by a smoking Good Lovin'/Not Fade Away/Drums sandwich and a scorching Viola Lee Blues. Jerry’s lead work is strong throughout, Phil’s vocals cut through despite mix issues, and Pigpen’s organ sits low but the rhythm section drives hard.",
    "review": "Port Chester’s Capitol Theater on March 20, 1970 captured an early‑Dead show that swings between tight acoustic storytelling and raw electric fire. The first set kicks off with a rousing Casey Jones and flows into the classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, then settles into blues‑laden numbers like Deep Elem Blues and Friend Of The Devil. After a brief acoustic interlude, the band erupts on the second set: a blistering Good Lovin' → Not Fade Away sandwich bookended by tight drumming, followed by an incendiary Viola Lee Blues that showcases both Jerry’s searing lead and the dual‑drum engine of Mickey and Bill. The night closes with High Time, Turn On Your Lovelight and a gentle “And We Bid You Good Night” encore, leaving listeners with a vivid snapshot of the Dead’s evolving sound in ’70.",
